#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186056008 :tutorial/content "To simplify an expression containing parentheses, we expand the parentheses and then collect like terms.\n\nFor example, let's simplify the following expression:\n\n$$\n3(x + 2) + 4x\n$$\n\nOur first step is to distribute the $3$ over each of the terms in the parentheses:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} 3(x + 2) + 4x \\\\[5pt] &= 3\\cdot x + 3\\cdot 2 + 4x \\\\[5pt] &= (3x) + (6) + 4x \\\\[5pt] &= 3x +6 + 4x & \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe second and final step is to collect the like terms:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} 3x + 6 + 4x \\\\ &= (3x + 4x) + 6 \\\\ &= 7x + 6 \\end{aligned}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "4f6f52d9-c069-5009-ae28-6eb58953a5cb" :tutorial/math-academy-id 8024 :tutorial/title "Introduction"}]}
